SCORE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=4 | implementability=4 | exploration_value=4
SCOPE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour is the top-8 weighted successor Karcher mean (E1-E3); the curved primitive is log_a and exp at the anchor and at every reconstructed point (E12-E13); the anchor enters the code-producing map itself; the three switches exist: a_i=0 removes behaviour, Euclidean exp/log removes curvature, both-off is the baseline quantizer. The specification never writes these switch values down.
EVIDENCE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | E1 E2 E3 E5 E10 E11 E12 E13
OBJECTION: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | A per-item anchor defines a different tangent frame at every item, yet the quantizer compares those tangent vectors to one shared codebook C_l with no parallel transport; log_{a_i} is only a local chart, so equal code tuples do not mean comparable displacements, and codebook gradients mix incomparable frames. The 0.5 s anchor clamp is also dimensionally ambiguous because the ball radius is 1 while s is a tangent-space scale.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | On the unit Poincare disk take a1=(0.9,0), a2=(-0.9,0) and the same tangent vector v=(0.1,0). exp_{a1}(v) lies near the right boundary and exp_{a2}(v) near the left boundary, so both items receive the same level-1 code while their hyperbolic distance is O(1); a per-item Euclidean origin shift has no such ambiguity because R^d tangent spaces are canonically identified by translation.
RATIONALE: behaviour_anchored_geodesic_charts | Behaviour 4: E5 measures the successor-entropy spread and E11 measures weak baseline prefix agreement (0.0907 vs null 0.067), so the SID hypothesis has measured headroom, but the claim that equal displacement from a personal anchor captures adjacency is not itself measured. Geometry 3: the curved maps sit inside the residual primitive, but a per-item Euclidean origin shift is a natural matched control reproducing the relative-displacement idea to first order, and chart non-commutativity is the only argued extra. Novelty 3: base-point selection differs from the ledger's tangent_successor_residual and successor_geodesic_coarse, but the write-up does not argue against them. Downstream 3: observables are concrete, yet relative charts can raise tuple collisions and the chain ignores that. Falsifiability 4: the behaviour-off, geometry-off and both-off arms are named with diagnostics and a criterion. Implementability 4: differentiable with stop-gradient anchors, clamped radius, frozen scale, plausible 1.05x cost; the tangent-frame issue is a correctness risk, not a missing implementation. Exploration 4. Uncertainty is single-run seed noise plus anchor stability; UNKNOWN is the fraction of empty or clamped anchors, resolvable by one pass over the frozen transition table and the epoch-0 anchor table.

SCORE: BRANCHCURV-SID | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=2 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: BRANCHCURV-SID | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour sets H_k and kappa_k and kappa_k enters the nearest-code distance and the soft assignment, so the pathway is connected; the neutral kappa_k=constant and the Euclidean limit are the switches, but they are not written down, so the three-comparison statement is missing from the text.
EVIDENCE: BRANCHCURV-SID | E1 E2 E3 E5 E10 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: BRANCHCURV-SID | u_i is built in the k0 ball but scored with d_{kappa_k} in the kappa_k ball; the denominator 1 - kappa_k||u_i||^2 is not guaranteed positive and no rescaling of u_i into the kappa_k ball is specified. The softmax assignment with tau fixed to the epoch-0 median distance also makes the 256 logits nearly uniform, so the quantizer may never commit.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: BRANCHCURV-SID | With k0=1, an item whose u_i has ||u_i||^2=0.9, and a code with kappa_k=4, the factor is 1-4(0.9)=-2.6; the arcosh argument can fall below 1 or the denominator changes sign, so the distance is undefined for a legal item-code pair and the level assignment breaks.
RATIONALE: BRANCHCURV-SID | Behaviour 4: E5 shows the hub/leaf spread (support 2.8 vs 44.3) and E10 documents coverage. Geometry 3: per-code curvature is a real curved object, but a per-code monotone rescaling of distance is close to a Euclidean reweighting and the claim that varied curvature buys resolution is not established. Novelty 3: per-code rather than per-item curvature, distinct from the ledger but a routine variation on the behaviour-sets-the-metric family. Downstream 3: entropy and prefix hit-rate are named, but near-uniform soft assignment can erase the token contrast the chain needs. Falsifiability 2: only the on-arm, no named off-arms or matched controls. Implementability 2: the curvature-ball mismatch needs an unspecified renormalisation, and the one-off tau is a hidden dependence. Exploration 3. UNKNOWN is the per-code entropy spread under epoch-0 assignments; the bounded check is to compute H_k for the 256 level-1 codes.

SCORE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=2 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=2
SCOPE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | IN_SCOPE | The base shift is inside the quantizer and the behaviour term shares u_hat with the fidelity term, so removing eta or replacing the maps with Euclidean ones changes the code chain; the three switch values are implicit (eta=0, Euclidean maps) and not stated.
EVIDENCE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: SUCC-TAN-RQ | This is a near-repeat of the ledger's tangent_successor_residual (E6 recall 0.055, T1 NLL 4.00): both shift the level-1 base along the mean successor tangent and then quantize. The eta derivation is also unstable: dividing by median||t_i|| scales every item to one code spacing, so items whose successor mean cancels receive a full-size shift in a noise direction.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | An item with two equally frequent successors placed symmetrically about u_i has log u_j1 - log u_i = -(log u_j2 - log u_i), so t_i=0 exactly and the behaviour shift vanishes; with a 51/49 split the same item receives a one-code-spacing shift along an almost arbitrary direction, so the mechanism is silent on the symmetric case and noise-driven on the near-symmetric case.
RATIONALE: SUCC-TAN-RQ | Behaviour 3: E5 shows successor concentration and direction exists, but the claim that residuals destroy direction is inferred, not measured. Geometry 2: exp/log is used, but a Euclidean tangent shift followed by a Euclidean residual reproduces the first-order behaviour, and E6 already shows l1_br_euclid at 0.06207 above the hyperbolic successors at 0.055. Novelty 2: a near-repeat not argued against the ledger. Downstream 3. Falsifiability 2: no named controls. Implementability 3: eta is derived, but boundary blow-up and the balance of the two reconstruction terms are unguarded. Exploration 2. UNKNOWN: the fraction of items with ||t_i|| below 10 percent of the median; the bounded check is one pass over the frozen successor table.

SCORE: GEOPFX-SID | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=4 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=4
SCOPE: GEOPFX-SID | IN_SCOPE | One loss couples transition pairs to the curved segment, so behaviour and geometry meet in the same term rather than in separate losses; the behaviour-off (w=0 or term off) and Euclidean-segment arms are implicit but not named.
EVIDENCE: GEOPFX-SID | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: GEOPFX-SID | The stated geometric premise is wrong: Euclidean space is a geodesic metric space with unique straight geodesics and a well-defined betweenness relation, so "Euclidean prefix codes have no betweenness" does not justify curvature. The term is an additive codebook regulariser, LCP_ij is a discrete function of the current assignment, and at initialisation almost all LCP are zero, so the term is inert until codes already agree.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GEOPFX-SID | For any three collinear Euclidean points x, g, y with g between x and y, the point-to-segment distance is zero, exactly as in the hyperbolic construction; a Euclidean point-to-segment loss on the same codebook has the same minimiser set. The only difference is the metric, not the existence of betweenness. Collapsing all prefix code vectors to one point also makes every g lie on every segment, a degenerate zero of the loss.
RATIONALE: GEOPFX-SID | Behaviour 3: E1/E2 give transitions and E11 shows the prefix signal is weak, but the off-geodesic displacement of prefix anchors is not measured. Geometry 3: a genuine point-to-segment hyperbolic distance, but the matched Euclidean segment distance is obvious and not argued away. Novelty 4: no ledger entry uses point-to-segment prefix alignment. Downstream 3: the beam-state story is plausible but the step from codebook geometry to generator prefix hit-rate is hand-waved. Falsifiability 2: only the on-arm. Implementability 3: twelve golden-section steps per pair and a min over s need an envelope or soft-min gradient that is not specified; the cost is affordable. Exploration 4. UNKNOWN: the epoch-0 delta distribution; bounded check over the sampled pairs.

SCORE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=4 | downstream_mechanism=2 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=2 | exploration_value=4
SCOPE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour sets atom positions inside the quantizer and the switches are uniform radius and Euclidean shells, but the write-up does not name the three comparisons.
EVIDENCE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E8 E10 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: GEO-SHELL-RQ | The radius formula is inverted relative to the hypothesis. r_lg=(N-B_g)/(N+B_g) sends the root (B_g=N) to rho=0 and a singleton leaf (B_g=1) to rho near the boundary, so broad communities occupy the small-radius shell and leaves the large-radius shell, the opposite of "broad behaviour communities will occupy large-radius shells". The projection half-width delta is also never defined, which is a free constant.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | For a balanced binary tree with N=100 leaves, the root has B=100 and r=0 so rho=0, while a leaf has B=1 and r=99/101=0.980 so rho=2 artanh(0.980)=4.60; the root is at the origin and the leaf near the boundary. The stated observable direction is refuted by the proposal's own formula before any training run.
RATIONALE: GEO-SHELL-RQ | Behaviour 3: E5 and E8 measure branching and the first-level capacity ceiling, but the Leiden leaf-mass distribution is not measured. Geometry 2: the radial map is a monotone reparameterisation of a Euclidean radius, so a Euclidean shell with radius artanh(r_lg) reproduces the radial ordering; only within-cone distances differ. Novelty 4: a closed-form tree embedding as atom placement is not in the ledger. Downstream 2: the direction from radius to token predictability is inverted and the generator sees token IDs, not radii. Falsifiability 2. Implementability 2: delta undefined and recursive Leiden plus shell projection need a redesign. Exploration 4. UNKNOWN: the B_g histogram of the frozen tree; bounded check is to build the tree once.

SCORE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | behavior_evidence=3 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=4 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=4 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | IN_SCOPE | Behaviour changes b_i inside d_c, so eta_i=0 and Euclidean interpolation are the two switches; the three comparisons are not named.
EVIDENCE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E10 E12 E13 E14 E15 E16
OBJECTION: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | The shift is largest exactly where the successor is most predictable: eta_i=c_i/(1+c_i) reaches 0.5 for a deterministic successor, so the item's reconstruction target moves halfway to its successor and can collide with the successor's own code, destroying the discrimination the generator needs.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | For an item i with one successor j and p_j=exp_{p_i}(v), b_i=exp_{p_i}(0.5v). If j is coded at p_j, the midpoint can be closer to j's code than to i's, so i and j can share a full SID; the pair then has no prefix discrimination and the generator cannot rank j above i, the opposite of the hypothesis.
RATIONALE: GYRO-TARGET-RQ | Behaviour 3: E5 measures successor concentration, but "items are interchangeable given the same successor set" is not measured. Geometry 3: a real geodesic interpolation, but the Euclidean interpolation is a matched control that differs only by curvature. Novelty 4: target-side coupling is not in the ledger. Downstream 3. Falsifiability 2: no named controls. Implementability 4: eta derived, stop-gradient table, negligible cost, eta<=0.5 keeps the map inside the ball. Exploration 3. UNKNOWN: the number of item pairs with identical successor sets; bounded check over the frozen table.

SCORE: KAPPA_BRANCH | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=3 | novelty=4 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=4
SCOPE: KAPPA_BRANCH | IN_SCOPE | h_i=0 gives constant kappa and kappa to 0 gives the Euclidean limit, so the switches exist inside the metric; their explicit values are not written down.
EVIDENCE: KAPPA_BRANCH | E1 E2 E3 E5 E10 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: KAPPA_BRANCH | The per-pair curvature is applied to points produced by per-item curvature maps: p(z_i) uses kappa_i, p(c_k) uses kappa_k, and d_kbar needs both points inside the kbar ball. For kappa_i=1 and kappa_k=2, kbar=4/3; any point with ||p||^2>3/4 gives 1-kbar||p||^2<0 and an undefined arcosh. The residual update r<-r-c is Euclidean while the assignment is curved, so the chain is a hybrid.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: KAPPA_BRANCH | kappa_i=1 and kappa_k=2 give kbar=4/3; take ||p(z_i)||^2=0.8, then 1-(4/3)(0.8)=-0.0667 and the distance denominator is negative for a legal item and code. Raising kappa from 1 to 2 also shrinks the ball radius from 1 to 1/sqrt(2), so "more curvature buys separation per bit" is not established by the volume argument.
RATIONALE: KAPPA_BRANCH | Behaviour 4: E5 is a strong measured hub/leaf stratification with coverage. Geometry 3: variable curvature is more than a constant-curvature control, but the variation is only a factor of two and the Euclidean or constant-kappa controls are close. Novelty 4: metric-side coupling with a derived field differs from the ledger's behaviour prior. Downstream 4: occupancy gap, first-token entropy and recall form a specific chain with a diagnostic. Falsifiability 2: no three named arms, only a conjunctive success criterion. Implementability 3: explicit formula and small cost, but the curvature-map mismatch and the missing numerical bound are real. Exploration 4. UNKNOWN: the fraction of items with kappa_i near 1 versus near 2; bounded check on the frozen pair counts.

SCORE: GEO_REPAIR | behavior_evidence=2 | geometry_necessity=2 | novelty=3 | downstream_mechanism=3 | falsifiability=2 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=3
SCOPE: GEO_REPAIR | OUT_OF_SCOPE | The encoder, codebooks, loss and quantizer distances are untouched; only the post-hoc assignment of already-colliding items changes, which is the excluded pattern of a behaviour suffix on an unchanged quantizer. What is missing is a coupled computation inside the quantizer, and the motivating collision rate is not in the bundle.
EVIDENCE: GEO_REPAIR | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E11 E12 E13 E14 E15
OBJECTION: GEO_REPAIR | This changes only the post-hoc assignment of items that already collide; the encoder, codebooks, loss and quantizer distances are untouched. The motivating collision rate is also not in the bundle, so the size of the repair set is unknown.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: GEO_REPAIR | With 24556 items and 256^3=16,777,216 full SID tuples, the baseline full-SID collision set can be empty; then G_s is a singleton for every s, the repair loop does nothing every epoch, and the predicted rise in distinct SIDs has no room to occur.
RATIONALE: GEO_REPAIR | Behaviour 2: E11 reports same-first-prefix 0.0907 but not full-SID collisions; the collision claim is high-risk and unmeasured. Geometry 2: Poincare distance is used only as a tie-break argmin over candidate codes, and a Euclidean argmin is a natural matched control. Novelty 3: collision repair is not in the ledger but the computation is small. Downstream 3: distinct SIDs and in-degree share are named, but generator reachability is not shown to follow. Falsifiability 2. Implementability 3: hash grouping and batched distances are cheap, but "free capacity" and the tie-break are undefined. Exploration 3. UNKNOWN: the full-SID collision rate; the bounded check is one pass over the frozen baseline item_sids to count duplicate tuples and the colliding tail.

SCORE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | behavior_evidence=4 | geometry_necessity=4 | novelty=4 | downstream_mechanism=4 | falsifiability=3 | implementability=3 | exploration_value=4
SCOPE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | IN_SCOPE | The cone hinge acts on the same centroids that produce the SID, and the behaviour-off and curvature-off removals are explicitly described; the trainable Lorentz curvature and cone order cannot be reproduced by a Euclidean quantizer.
EVIDENCE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | E1 E2 E3 E5 E6 E10 E12 E13 E14
OBJECTION: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | The future-cone order is transitive and antisymmetric, so requiring every observed transition pair to be timelike future-ordered is infeasible whenever the training transition graph has a 2-cycle or a longer cycle, which session co-consumption commonly produces; the hinge then has no zero-loss configuration and the curvature and codebooks oscillate.
COUNTEREXAMPLE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | Take training transitions i->j and j->i with M_l>0. The hinge requires both e_j-e_i and e_i-e_j to be timelike future, i.e. (e_j)_0>(e_i)_0 and (e_i)_0>(e_j)_0, which cannot both hold for distinct points, and the strict time ordering fails if the points coincide. Any reciprocal pair makes the cone loss unsatisfiable at zero, so the cone violation rate cannot be driven to zero.
RATIONALE: LORENTZ_CONE_RQ | Behaviour 4: E5 and E6 show successor structure and the collapse of level-2/3 prefix survival, the problem the cone is meant to fix. Geometry 4: trainable Lorentz curvature plus a causal cone is genuinely curved with no Euclidean counterpart, and removing either behaviour or curvature changes the centroids. Novelty 4: no ledger entry uses causal order. Downstream 4: cone violation, level-2 predictability and recall form a specific chain. Falsifiability 3: behaviour-off and curvature-off removals are discussed and a failure criterion is given, but matched controls are not all named. Implementability 3: log/exp and the hinge are cheap, but cycle infeasibility, the unspecified Frechet-style centroid update and boundary blow-up are unresolved. Exploration 4. UNKNOWN: the fraction of reciprocal or cyclic transition pairs; bounded check on the frozen transition graph.